  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, pleasant weather continues through the \nwork week as temperatures warm up.\n\nLarge high pressure continues to be the primary driver of the \npleasant days and cool nights through Thursday. Expecting \nanother night of radiational cooling dropping temperatures; \nhowever, party cloudy skies heading into the overnight hours \nwill likely hinder the cooling effect... expecting temperatures \nto remain in the upper 30s to low 40s.\n\nSouthern New England will see warmer temperatures Thursday and \nFriday as a surge of warm air advection ahead of a cold front \nwarms 925 mb temperatures to between +11 and +15C... highs \nThursday in the low to mid 60s, upper 60s for the Merrimack and \nConnecticut River Valleys, and highs Friday near 70 for the \nsouthern and eastern coastal plains (mid 60s for the interior \nand Cape/Islands). As the cold front approaches Friday, winds \nare likely to be gustier than we've seen the last several \ndays...likely 20 to 25 mph at times, possibly 30 to 35 mph in \nthe higher elevations.\n\nPost-FROPA Friday night, skies clear and winds calm for another\nnight of radiational cooling. Temperatures will once again drop\ninto the low to mid 30s for most.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...High pressure likely to maintain dry weather \nthrough at least Monday. A slight risk for some showers Tuesday \ninto Wednesday, but the details are not yet certain.\n\nThe dominant high pressure should finally move off the coast \nSunday night into Monday. Even then, its influence is expected \nto slowly diminish into the middle of next week.\n\nThere are rather large discrepancies in the evolution of a mid \nlevel ridge across the central USA early next week. The \nmagnitude and orientation of this ridge will be the determining \nfactor for the track of a low pressure trying to move east from \nthe Rockies. at this time, thinking this low pressure will \nstruggle to move east quickly. That leaves the positioning of \nits associated fronts to consider. With the low pressure quite a\nways away from southern New England, there will not be much \npush to move them much as well.\n\nOverall, the most likely outcome is that dry weather prevails. \nHowever, a quasi-warm front may get close enough to our region \nTuesday into Wednesday to perhaps trigger a few showers. Not \nseeing much moisture to work with, so any precipitation would be\nlight.",
